WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:04.130
Start with two rectangles, each six centimetres by four.

00:00:04.580 --> 00:00:07.290
Cut a one centimetre square from a corner of the first,

00:00:07.580 --> 00:00:10.080
and a one centimetre square from the middle of an edge

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:10.830
of the second.

00:00:11.320 --> 00:00:12.730
What are the two new perimeters?

00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:20.360
Each original rectangle has perimeter six plus four plus

00:00:20.360 --> 00:00:22.870
six plus four: twenty centimetres.

00:00:23.930 --> 00:00:26.690
The corner cut removes two one-centimetre pieces of the

00:00:26.690 --> 00:00:30.120
old boundary, and exposes two new one-centimetre pieces.

00:00:30.670 --> 00:00:31.660
The change is zero.

00:00:31.990 --> 00:00:33.740
The perimeter is still twenty centimetres.

00:00:34.820 --> 00:00:37.760
The cut in the middle of an edge removes only one piece

00:00:37.760 --> 00:00:40.630
of old boundary, but exposes three new pieces.

00:00:41.300 --> 00:00:42.690
The change is plus two.

00:00:43.300 --> 00:00:45.270
The perimeter is twenty-two centimetres.

00:00:46.500 --> 00:00:48.270
Both shapes have lost the same area.

00:00:48.680 --> 00:00:51.410
Each has area twenty-three square centimetres.

00:00:51.560 --> 00:00:53.010
Yet their perimeters differ.

00:00:53.660 --> 00:00:56.140
The area removed does not decide the change in

00:00:56.140 --> 00:00:58.510
perimeter; the position of the cut does.

00:00:59.460 --> 00:01:03.000
New perimeter equals old perimeter, minus the boundary

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:05.350
removed, plus the boundary created.

00:01:05.820 --> 00:01:08.700
Rebuild this, then find the perimeter when a two by one

00:01:08.700 --> 00:01:10.840
rectangle is cut from the middle of an edge,

00:01:10.840 --> 00:01:12.830
with its long side along the edge.
